  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Rain showers arrive in the mountains late\ntonight. Limited chances east of the mountains into Saturday.\n\nModels are in close agreement that the first front arrives later\ntonight with rain showers pushing into the mountains by dawn.\n\nThere will be several shortwaves pivoting around a deep trough \nin the east into Friday night. This first wave with the front \nshould reach the Piedmont Thursday early afternoon. Could be \nenough ample lift with the upper support for widely scattered \nshowers developing or moving into the Piedmont, but pattern \nappears such that we see a jump from the better shower chances \nin the mountains to a \"rain shadow\" of sorts across the \nfoothills.\n\nThe next front and shortwave starts introducing colder air\nThursday evening where we see any rain change to snow showers in\nthe higher elevations after midnight. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Colder air rushes in Thursday night into Friday \nwith light accumulations of snow in the higher elevations, with \nflurries as far east as the I-81 corridor possible.\n\nBest upper support and strong cold advection should push into\nthe area late Thursday night into Friday with accumulating snow\nshowers at times into the higher elevations. With ground temps\nwarm and the snow being wind blow and episodic in nature,\naccumulations look to be on the lighter side. Could very well be\na case where some get a dusting to an inch or so, mainly grassy\nsurfaces/rooftops, then once the precip stops it melts. Total\nQPF pushes some snowfall totals closer to 2-3 inches in the\nhigher terrain of Greenbrier and maybe Beech Mountain, but again\nthat would be a total snowfall, not equating to snow depth.\n\nHence at this time, not seeing any need for advisories.\n\nThe caboose of the upper trough/shortwave pushes east Saturday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Temperatures stay colder than normal into early \nnext week then gradually return to normal by the middle to end \nof next week.\n\nAfter the being used to mild to warm weather aside from some\nchilly overnights, highs Friday into the weekend will be\nrunning as much as 15 to 20 degrees below normal mainly in the\nmountains and 5 t 10 degrees below normal in the Piedmont. The\nwinds should keep the air mixed where overnight lows will be\njust below normal.\n\nWith the winds staying up, wind chills should be as cold as the\nteens to lower 20s from the mountains to Piedmont Friday night,\nand just a few degrees higher Sat night.\n\nThe upper flow splits into next week with the deeper/colder\ntrough lifting northeast, but our area will remain in an upper\ntrough into Tuesday before ridging aloft arrives mid to late\nweek. Surface high will be north of us into Wednesday keeping \nus dry and seasonably cool.",
